-- Lesson 1: Greater Than, Less Than
'{"hook":"The symbols < and > are used billions of times a day in computer code, thermostats, and speed limits. They were invented by a man who helped colonize Virginia.","inventor":{"name":"Thomas Harriot","birth_year":1560,"death_year":1621,"nationality":"English","bio":"An English astronomer, mathematician, and navigator who sailed to Virginia with Sir Walter Raleigh. He introduced the < and > symbols in his posthumous work Artis Analyticae Praxis (1631), published ten years after his death."},"year_invented":1631,"etymology":{"word":"inequality","from":"Latin","parts":[{"root":"in-","meaning":"not"},{"root":"aequalis","meaning":"equal"}]},"story_paragraphs":["Before Thomas Harriot, mathematicians wrote inequalities in words: ''A is greater than B'' or ''C is less than D.'' Just as Robert Recorde had simplified ''is equal to'' into =, someone needed to do the same for ''is greater than'' and ''is less than.''","Harriot, who was both a serious mathematician and a real-world explorer (he mapped parts of Virginia and learned the Algonquian language), developed the symbols < and > in his mathematical work. Sadly, they were only published in 1631, a decade after his death.","The symbols are elegantly designed: the wide end points to the bigger number, the pointed end to the smaller. Think of it as a hungry alligator — it always opens its mouth toward the bigger meal.","Today, < and > are everywhere. Every ''if'' statement in computer programming uses them. Your thermostat uses them to decide when to turn on the heating. Speed limits, age restrictions, weight limits — inequalities rule the real world."],"key_contributors":[{"name":"Pierre Bouguer","contribution":"Introduced the ≤ and ≥ symbols (less than or equal, greater than or equal) in 1734"},{"name":"Augustin-Louis Cauchy","contribution":"Made rigorous use of inequalities foundational to mathematical analysis"}],"real_world_applications":[{"name":"Speed Limits","icon":"🚗","description":"Speed ≤ 60 km/h means your speed must be less than or equal to 60"},{"name":"Age Restrictions","icon":"🎬","description":"Age ≥ 13 means you must be 13 or older"},{"name":"Programming","icon":"💻","description":"Every if/else statement in code uses < > ≤ ≥ to make decisions"},{"name":"Temperature Control","icon":"🌡️","description":"Thermostats use inequalities: if temp < 20°C, turn on heating"}],"image":{"url":null,"alt":"Artis Analyticae Praxis by Thomas Harriot","attribution":"Wikimedia Commons, public domain","source_url":null}}'::jsonb)
